  "body": "<p>Serath follows the golden footprints because someone is hurt. <a href=\"#modeli\">Modeli</a> wants to know what left them. <a href=\"#thorn\">Thorn</a> arrives on Toadimus and finds her magic becoming unreliable. None of them begins the journey expecting to choose which companion will leave the First Forest alive.</p><h2 id=\"serath-golden-trail\">The blood in the snow</h2><p>Dragos finds delicate golden footprints leading into the First Forest. The company follows them through a snow squall, losing the trail and finding drops again. Modeli insists on examining the gold rather than following a mystery they have not tried to understand. Serath wants to reach whoever has been wounded.</p><p>Magic is already going wrong. A spell meant to protect Thorn instead leaves her sick; another drives her into an attack on Arethusa. Modeli and Serath help the fallen sorceress. Thorn’s anger subsides, and she is shaken by what she has done. The danger is not confined to the creatures coming out of the trees.</p><p>Serath then collapses after trying to heal herself. Thorn rushes to help. Modeli reaches for an answer from the stories he has read: a kiss that might work where familiar magic cannot. Under the distorted weave she gets up again. They continue toward the light together. That strange reprieve is still recent when they meet the person whose blood they followed.</p><h2 id=\"serath-wounded-caretaker\">The goddess cannot protect herself</h2><p>A woman comes forward with cracks running through her body and golden ichor leaking from them. She coughs, almost falls and grows grayer. She names herself Aionia, goddess of time, space and the structure of reality. She says the failing weave is leaking from her with her blood.</p><p>Aionia tells them Vain defeated her, that Death took another tree and that a Shard was devoured. Those events reach this company through her account. The visible problem is immediate: she is spent, something follows her, and she says even an ordinary warrior could now strike her down.</p><p>Thorn asks what is hunting her. When Aionia asks for the Great Stag, Thorn names herself a former archdruid of the Feywild Moon and offers to try. Modeli asks whether he serves her. Aionia’s answer lets him recognize the patron behind his own magic. Serath gives her full name—Serath Vareth—and offers to share the goddess’s burden.</p><p>The company does not agree about helping. Aleister accuses the gods of lying and speaks of the world and family he left behind. Arethusa wants Aionia to yield her place to Umberlee. Serath and Modeli put themselves between the weakened goddess and those approaching her.</p><h2 id=\"serath-takes-the-shard\">The blow meant for Aionia</h2><p>Aleister throws a purple Delerium shard at the kneeling goddess. Serath leaps into its path. The shard enters her chest, and Aionia screams with her. Serath staggers but stays in the way, telling him to stop.</p><p>Modeli threatens to fight anyone who touches Aionia and tries to cover her with his own body. His warning about the Wyrm is an attempt to make the others stop, based on what the goddess has just told them. It does not mean he has witnessed the destruction he fears.</p><p>Thorn changes into a stag and calls for the Great Stag, pushing through the sickness left by the broken magic. The calls do not bring immediate rescue. Something hears, but another threat is approaching too: a violet cloud that comes for Aionia.</p><h2 id=\"serath-falling-goddess\">They cannot hold the mist away</h2><p>Modeli tries to help Aionia walk, then steps toward the cloud with his amulet held out. He hopes his own magic can draw the attack instead. Serath refuses to leave him fighting alone. Thorn, still in stag form, offers another way to carry the goddess away.</p><p>The mist reaches Aionia. Modeli prays and tries to strengthen her. Serath summons a weapon, surrounds the struggle with darkness and keeps herself between the goddess and the attackers. She explains her choice simply: Aionia is bleeding for others, and that is a wound she cannot ignore.</p><p>The clouds drain Aionia’s grace and fill her lungs. She falls. For a moment the weave returns to normal, and Modeli feels his newly recognized patron die. The company has not protected her by wanting it badly enough. Serath turns to the life-tether ankh she carries.</p><h2 id=\"serath-life-for-life\">The offer costs the person making it</h2><p>Serath lowers herself beside Aionia and places the ankh on her. She calls on those she worships and offers herself if the goddess can live. The first attempt fails. The narrator warns that another attempt—or another soul—could cost everything.</p><p>Thorn offers her mind, body, soul and magic. Modeli begs for Aionia’s return even if it means his own destruction. Their willingness is real, but the narrated outcome is specific: neither of their souls is enough in those attempts. Both remain alive. Serath tries again.</p><p>Her soul enters Aionia’s body. Color and life return to the goddess as Serath slumps to the ground. Aionia cradles her and cries. The restoration has a named cost, borne by the person who took the earlier blow.</p><p>The Great Stag has finally appeared on the horizon in answer to Thorn. When it joins Aionia, the goddess recovers her form and receives its strength. Together they ride back into the storm and the battle. Serath’s transfer and the Stag’s later aid are distinct parts of that return.</p><h2 id=\"serath-the-body-brought-home\">The lesson Modeli cannot easily accept</h2><p>Aionia turns to Aleister and brings him back to life. She calls this her first lesson to Modeli: people who deserve death may receive another chance, while better people are taken too soon. She asks him to remember Serath and remember why he fights.</p><p>Modeli promises to preserve her words, but he also blames himself for failing to save Serath. His guilt does not settle responsibility for the death. He lifts her body and starts home toward Pristinia, furious that Aleister has received the chance she has lost. Thorn lays a hand on Serath and prays through tears that her name will endure in water, wind and memory.</p><p>The loss does not end with the quest. Rumors trouble Pristinia the next day, and Arethusa repeats her opposition to the sacrifice as her own judgment. Later Modeli watches over the body and hopes for a revival. On the journey into The Before he still thinks of Serath lying dead while Aleister walks free. No later restoration is supplied by those scenes.</p><p>For Thorn, the memory includes a call finally answered and a companion she could not bring home alive. For Modeli, discovering his goddess becomes inseparable from the life spent returning her to the war. <a href=\"#thorn\">Read Thorn’s history</a> · <a href=\"#modeli\">Read Modeli’s history</a> · <a href=\"#aionia\">Aionia</a> · <a href=\"#first-forest\">The First Forest</a></p><p><a href=\"#serath-vareth\">Read Serath Vareth’s life before the sacrifice →</a></p>",
